course: dictionary
lesson: 14758
title: Proposition: an offer, a statement or a challenge
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: A proposition is a word with several meanings, so the clues around it matter. In business, a proposition is a plan or offer that someone suggests. «I have a proposition for you: let's run a bake sale and share the profit.» The words share the profit show that it is a plan to think about.
explain: In maths and logic, a proposition is a statement that is either true or false. «7 is odd» is a true proposition, and «7 is even» is a false one. When you meet the word in a puzzle, ask yourself: can I check this and say true or false?
explain: The word can also mean a task you have to deal with. «That hill is a tough proposition» means the hill is hard to climb, not that someone made an offer. Swap your meaning into the sentence to check it still makes sense.
fact: A proposition is an idea, plan or offer that is suggested.
fact: In maths and logic, a proposition is a statement that is either true or false.
fact: «A business proposition» means a plan that could make money.
fact: «An attractive proposition» means an offer that is appealing and worth wanting.
fact: «A tough proposition» means a difficult task or thing to deal with.
exercise: «I have a proposition for you: let's start a bake sale.» In this sentence, what is a proposition? | *A plan or offer that is suggested | A kind of cake | A true statement about numbers | A hill that is hard to climb
exercise: Which of these is a proposition in maths? | Close the door, please | Wow, what a day! | *«7 is odd» | What time is it?
exercise: «That hill is a tough proposition.» What does tough proposition mean here? | A cheerful picnic spot | A plan to sell the hill | *A hard task to deal with | A true statement about the hill
test: Mia says «7 is even» is a proposition. Is she right? | Yes, because it is an order | No, because it is a question | No, because it is false | *Yes, because it is a statement that is either true or false
test: «The new job is a different proposition from the old one.» What does a different proposition mean here? | A different number | Exactly the same job | A different plan for a bake sale | *A different kind of challenge
exam: Which meaning fits «a business proposition»? | A kind of sandwich | A statement that is always false | Two sides of a river | *A plan or offer suggested for making money
